  "design": "Written for the open pack; every screen is drawn here from the towns' geography (tables/towns.py) and the open battle sheet's own tiles (art/battle.png, its terrain masks art/battle-terrain.png at 32 x 32 pixels a tile, and its ground in art/battle.tiles), read from the directory in TILE_ART, or else from the art/ of the pack opendata.py writes. Nothing comes from the original game. How the engine reads them (src/sim/battle.c, land.c). A fort battle is fought on the sea screen of the town's id, with the town's fort and ship cells (data/towns/<year>); the colour under a ship's centre (its start cell plus 40, 30 pixels) is sea, shallows (they scrape a hull) or land (it beaches the ship, or lands the men when the fort is within reach). Other sea battles take a set by the land seen from the fleet (north, east, south, west, or none: open) and a random piece 0-3 of it; scrolling along the coast steps to the next piece, (piece + scroll) & 3, and scrolling off it to piece 4. A land battle is fought on the land screen of the town's id: the town's men stand at its land cell and on the clear cells around it, the attackers come ashore at its party cell and the cells diagonally beside it. A tile's ground (clear, cover, swamp, water) sets the troops' speed, cover halves the losses and hides a unit, water doubles them; nothing blocks the way. The towns. Each town's cells follow from the direction of its water (towns.battle_cells(), the same in every era, which the checks below confirm): on the sea screen the shore runs across that direction just seaward of the fort, so the fort tile (123) stands on the land with the waterline less than a cell beyond it, the town's house (124) at the land cell behind it and more houses around by the town's size (its most citizens in any era), and the ship starts on open water with the way to the fort and to the screen's edge clear. On the land screen the shore lies past the party and the men beside it, so they land on clear ground with the town ahead of them across land, the fort tile again at the fort cell. Away from those cells the shore wanders by a seeded noise: headlands, bays, a river mouth, offshore cays. Each town also gets a character from its geography: the Main's and Central America's ports jungle (woods, a river), the great Spanish ports cleared land and a bigger town, the large islands' towns mixed woods and fields, the small islands open ground with cays offshore, the dry coast (Coro, Araya, Curacao, Bonaire, Margarita) rock and scrub, Florida and the Carolinas marsh and pine woods with barrier cays, Maracaibo's narrows and Gibraltar's lake shore marsh and pools (Maracaibo with the far shore of the strait in the corners), the inland towns no sea at all on their land screen (Granada its lake), Caracas among rocks, Puerto Principe on open plain. The seed is the town's id, so no two towns look alike. The sets. Screens 65-89: north 65-69, east 70-74, south 75-79, west 80-84 (pieces 0-3 a coast, piece 4 open water with a few cays), open 85-89 (open sea, no land: the set of a fleet out of sight of land). Each coast is one strip four screens long, cyclic, drawn and tiled as a whole and cut into its pieces, so a piece's edge runs on into the next piece's and the fourth's into the first's; the land keeps to the side the set is named for, within the outer 5 cells, clear of where the ships start (the field's middle). Screens 90-107 are open sea. The land screens 65-107 belong to no town; they are open country with a village, valid but unused. Tiling. Each screen (or strip) is designed as a terrain raster first, one class per pixel (sea, shallows, land; a 6 pixel shallows halo along every shore, as the art paints it), and a ground plan per cell (plain, woods, marsh, rock, house, fort). Cells all land take the plan's tile, cells all water a sea tile (a few with white caps far from land); the coast cells take the coast tile (128-254, and near a town the shore houses 68-79) whose mask best matches the raster, pixel by pixel, and then, among those within a tolerance, the one whose edges best match its four neighbours' (iterated conditional modes, as tables/world_map.py does for the sailing map), so a coastline runs on across tile borders. A design that fails the checks is drawn again with the next seed. Checks (the build stops on a failure): every town in every era has the same cells; on its sea screen the fort cell holds the fort tile, a ship can reach land within the fort's reach from its start, through water, and also the screen's edge, the start is open sea, and the land cell holds a house; on its land screen the land cell holds a house, the party's three cells are on the field, the party cell clear and the others dry, joined to the town over dry ground, and the town's men all find clear cells on the field; each coast piece has land along its named edge, open sea where the ships start and none on the far half, and the four pieces join; the open set has no land nor shallows. 65 towns, each checked on both screens (1 designs drawn again); coast pieces join within 30 pixels of 480."
